## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests mastery of DAG shortest paths and cross-checks understanding of BFS/DFS-derived ordering vs Dijkstra's heap-based approach.

## Key Concepts
- Topological sort (DFS or Kahn)
- DAG single-source shortest paths via DP
- Edge relaxation and cycle detection

## Code Example
```javascript
function dagSSSP(n, edges, s){
  const g=[...Array(n)].map(()=>[]);
  const indeg=new Array(n).fill(0);
  for(const [u,v,w] of edges){g[u].push([v,w]); indeg[v]++;}
  const q=[]; for(let i=0;i<n;i++) if(indeg[i]===0) q.push(i);
  const topo=[]; while(q.length){ const u=q.shift(); topo.push(u); for(const [v] of g[u]){ if(--indeg[v]===0) q.push(v);} }
  const INF=1e15; const dist=new Array(n).fill(INF); dist[s]=0;
  for(const u of topo){ if(dist[u]===INF) continue; for(const [v,w] of g[u]) dist[v]=Math.min(dist[v], dist[u]+w);} 
  return dist;
}
```

